WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
There are moments in a nation's history that matter more than all the battles.

2
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:11.380
Moments when a people rises from a tribe to a kingdom.

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.460
Moments when its ruler stops being merely a prince and becomes a king.

4
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:26.720
Moments when a golden crown arrives from a distant capital, and with it comes what no Serbian ruler had before.

5
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.260
Status, recognition, a place at the table of Europe's great kingdoms.

6
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.900
For our people such a moment came in 1077.

7
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.800
Then from Rome, from the hands of Pope Gregory VII himself, a crown arrived.

8
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.880
And the man who placed this crown on his head was Mihailo Vojislavljević.

9
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.680
The first Serbian king in history.

10
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.060
But before Mihailo there was his father, Stefan Vojislav.

11
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.020
The man who first lit the flame that later turned into a royal fire.

12
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.420
The man who in a single night defeated the strongest army of the time.

13
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.140
This is the story of the Vojislavljević family.

14
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.480
About the people who raised Duklja from the ashes after the death of John Vladimir.

15
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:30.220
About the war against Byzantium, about the blood that soaked the hills above Bar, and about the crown that traveled from Rome to finally adorn a Serbian head.

16
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:36.420
After the martyr's death of John Vladimir in 1016, Duklja fell under foreign rule.

17
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:45.920
The Byzantine emperor Basil II, the very Bulgar-slayer who defeated Samuel's army, now completed the conquest of the Balkans.

18
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.820
Bulgaria fell, all of Macedonia was returned under Byzantine administration,

19
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.500
and Duklja, left without its young king and without its own dynasty,

20
00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.000
became just another province of the great thousand-year-old empire.

21
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:01.780
Almost half a century.

22
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.520
That is how long Byzantine rule over the Serbian coast lasted.

23
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.660
Half a century during which our ancestors paid taxes to Constantinople,

24
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.700
received Byzantine governors, watched their land become part of a foreign administration.

25
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:21.740
But the blood of the Vlastimirovićs, the blood of the first Serbian princes, did not die out.

26
00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:27.400
Somewhere in the mountains, in small regions, in surviving noble families

27
00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.100
the blood flowed on. And waited for its hour.

28
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:37.480
That hour came with a man whose name would become a legend — Stefan Vojislav.

29
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.480
Stefan Vojislav was a nobleman from the Duklja region.

30
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.640
According to one tradition, he was a descendant of John Vladimir, perhaps through his sister or a relative.

31
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.320
According to another tradition, he traced his lineage to one of the ancient Serbian noble families

32
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.920
that survived the Byzantine conquest.

33
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.760
One way or another, he carried within him memory and rebellion.

34
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.740
Sometime in the 1030s Stefan Vojislav began to resist Byzantine rule.

35
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.460
First quietly, then louder.

36
00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.420
And in 1034 he attempted an open uprising, but unsuccessfully.

37
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.580
The Byzantines took him prisoner and carried him off to Constantinople.

38
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.860
There he spent years in confinement, but — and this is special in his story — managed to escape.

39
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.100
He returned to Duklja. He returned as a man who had seen Constantinople from the inside,

40
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.660
who understood both its strength and its weaknesses.

41
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:40.140
And no longer a nobleman who had raised a rebellion, but a leader to whom the people returned.

42
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.060
Around 1040 Stefan Vojislav raised an uprising again.

43
00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.040
This time successfully.

44
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.580
He drove out the Byzantine governors, took Skadar, the most important city of the region,

45
00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.720
and declared Duklja an independent state.

46
00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.120
Byzantium had to respond.

47
00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:05.580
In 1042 the Byzantine emperor Constantine IX sent a huge army to suppress the uprising.

48
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.860
At its head stood the commander Michael, governor of Dyrrachium.

49
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.960
With him, according to Byzantine sources, marched 60,000 warriors — an almost unthinkable number for that time.

50
00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:25.660
The army was to cross the mountainous terrain, enter the heart of Duklja and eliminate Stefan Vojislav once and for all.

51
00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.720
And Stefan Vojislav?

52
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:34.360
He had only a few thousand men, maybe five, maybe seven, no one knows exactly.

53
00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:39.620
But we know there were desperately few: one to ten, and possibly even more.

54
00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.680
An open battle would have been suicide.

55
00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:46.260
Stefan Vojislav didn't plan an open battle.

56
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.420
The plan was different. The plan was to use the land.

57
00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:56.460
The Byzantine army passed through the hills near Bar and camped in the valley above the river.

58
00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:00.760
It was confident, it was numerous, it was overconfident.

59
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:10.200
But, like the Bulgarians in the time of Vlastimir, centuries earlier, this army was on foreign soil, in mountains it did not know.

60
00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:15.600
And Stefan Vojislav knew every stone, every stream, every path.

61
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:25.120
That night, probably October 7, 1042, Stefan Vojislav moved on the Byzantine camp with all his men.

62
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.520
Quietly, through the hills, he surrounded them from all sides.

63
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.940
His sons — Mihailo, Gojslav, Predimir and others — led separate detachments.

64
00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.080
And then the attack began.

65
00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:44.280
The Byzantine army, caught by surprise, disorganized, in panic, had no chance.

66
00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.600
The cavalry couldn't turn around in the narrow mountain gorges.

67
00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.680
Arrows fell on the camp from all sides.

68
00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.400
The warriors tried to defend themselves, to flee, but didn't know where.

69
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:05.420
Later sources say that in that single night 40,000 Byzantine warriors died or were captured.

70
00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.760
Commander Michael himself barely escaped alive.

71
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:14.540
It was one of the greatest victories in all of Serbian medieval history.

72
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.980
And thanks to it Duklja became a fact.

73
00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:22.000
Byzantium never again tried to win it back.

74
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.300
Stefan Vojislav died a few years after this glorious victory.

75
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.880
He left behind five sons who divided power,

76
00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.860
as was the Slavic custom.

77
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:38.500
But one of them, Mihailo, gradually took precedence over the other brothers.

78
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:41.920
Mihailo was a different kind of ruler than his father.

79
00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.200
The father was a warrior, Mihailo a diplomat.

80
00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:49.680
The father built the state with the sword, Mihailo built it with marriage,

81
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.160
alliances and a careful game with Europe's strongest centers.

82
00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:59.160
He married a Byzantine princess, the daughter of a relative of the Byzantine emperor.

83
00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.600
Thus he tied his royal blood to the imperial blood of Constantinople,

84
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.980
and then began to look even further — to the west, to Rome.

85
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.640
At that time Rome and Constantinople were in an ever-deepening split.

86
00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:18.940
The Great Schism of 1054 divided Christianity into two parts:

87
00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.640
Orthodoxy in the east and Catholicism in the west.

88
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.900
Pope Gregory VII, one of the most powerful popes in history,

89
00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.520
was building his network of influence in all parts of Europe that could join him.

90
00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:34.820
And Mihailo saw an opportunity.

91
00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:41.580
He wrote to Rome, made alliances, promised church allegiance and, most important,

92
00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:46.380
asked for what no Serbian ruler before him had — he asked for a crown.

93
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.280
Pope Gregory VII agreed.

94
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.660
In 1077 a golden crown arrived in Duklja from Rome.

95
00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.980
The coronation ceremony took place, and Mihailo Vojislavljević became the first Serbian king.

96
00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.200
Not a prince, not an archon, but a king.

97
00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:06.880
It was a historic turning point.

98
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:12.900
Serbia now, formally, before the whole Christian world, had become a kingdom.

99
00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:18.080
On a par with Hungary, with Poland, with other kingdoms of Europe.

100
00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.700
Mihailo's son, Constantine Bodin, continued his father's line,

101
00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:26.540
fighting against Byzantium and expanding the borders in all directions.

102
00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:31.260
And then, in his time, the Vojislavljevićs reached their zenith.

103
00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:38.900
Duklja encompassed not only its coastal lands, but also Raška, Bosnia and even parts of present-day Albania.

104
00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:47.020
It was the largest Serbian state since Časlav and, for the first time in our history, it was a kingdom.

105
00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:56.740
After the death of Constantine Bodin in the early 12th century the Vojislavljević dynasty entered a period when brothers raised swords against each other.

106
00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.220
As before, as after Vlastimir, as after Časlav,

107
00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:05.980
the Slavic custom of dividing power among relatives brings new conflicts,

108
00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.460
new betrayals, new coups.

109
00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:13.700
The center of Serbian power shifts again, slowly but surely,

110
00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:18.360
from the coast to the inner lands, from Duklja to Raška.

111
00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:24.160
And in Raška, on the threshold of the 12th century, a new family begins to grow,

112
00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:30.900
a family whose name will mark the next 200 years of Serbian history — the Vukanovićs.

113
00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:39.760
And from this family there will soon emerge a man whose name will become the best known in all of Serbian medieval history.

114
00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.240
A man who will unite the scattered Serbian lands.

115
00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:48.780
A man who will lay the foundations of the greatest dynasty of our people — the Nemanjićs.

116
00:09:48.780 --> 00:10:01.920
But before Stefan Nemanja comes, we need to understand the chaos that followed, a period of coups, conflicts, short-lived rulers, a time that prepared the ground for his arrival.

117
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:12.680
About this, about the pre-Nemanjić era, about Tihomir and his brothers, about the moment when the rise of the Nemanjićs was only taking shape, we'll tell in the next episode.

118
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:16.640
If the story of the Vojislavljevićs moved you,

119
00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.900
if you understood what value this one crown from Rome had,

120
00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:22.720
subscribe to the channel.

121
00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.200
Hit the bell.

122
00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:31.100
Share this episode with everyone who thinks that Serbs got a royal crown only in the time of the Nemanjićs.

123
00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:33.140
Next episode.

124
00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:34.740
Before the Nemanjić era.

125
00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:38.680
Chaos, coups and the arrival of Tihomir and Nemanja.

126
00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.080
And until then —

127
00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.280
remember Stefan Vojislav,

128
00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:48.540
the man who with a few thousand men defeated an imperial army of 60 thousand.

129
00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:55.100
And remember Mihailo, the man who brought the first Serbian royal crown from Rome,

130
00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.000
because behind every king stands a father who earned the crown.